      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Scottish quarry workers dubbed the local sandstone pits Paradise, Purgatory and Hell Hole, a quirky legacy still remembered today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrmont%2C_New_South_Wales",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1920s, a former quarry site became the Maybanke Free Kindergarten, honoring feminist educator Maybanke Anderson.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrmont%2C_New_South_Wales",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pyrmont once powered Sydney’s waterfront with wharves, shipyards and woolstores, before turning into a media and IT hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
